<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Herophilus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 335 – c. 280 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Herophilus of Chalcedon was a Greek physician who worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> under the early Ptolemies and is often called the father of anatomy. He was among the first to perform systematic dissections of human bodies, distinguished nerves from tendons, described the brain, the eye and the duodenum, and studied the pulse, founding a medical school whose influence lasted for centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/herophilus.jpg" alt="Herophilus" width="320" height="316" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ecelan · CC BY-SA 3.0 es · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zaragoza_-_Antigua_Facultad_de_Medicina_-_Medall%C3%B3n_-_Her%C3%B3filo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
